# Licensing

crosscheck is **not licensed as a single unit**. The parts that run inside your
company are permissively licensed; the hub — the part someone would resell — is
not. Which file governs a given source file is decided by the package it lives
in, never by this one.

| Path | License | File |
|---|---|---|
| `packages/connector-claude/` | Apache License 2.0 | [`packages/connector-claude/LICENSE`](packages/connector-claude/LICENSE) |
| `packages/connector-core/` | Apache License 2.0 | [`packages/connector-core/LICENSE`](packages/connector-core/LICENSE) |
| `packages/connector-acp/` | Apache License 2.0 | [`packages/connector-acp/LICENSE`](packages/connector-acp/LICENSE) |
| `packages/schema/` | Apache License 2.0 | [`packages/schema/LICENSE`](packages/schema/LICENSE) |
| `packages/server/` | Functional Source License 1.1 (ALv2 Future License) | [`packages/server/LICENSE`](packages/server/LICENSE) |
| everything else (docs, CI, tooling) | Apache License 2.0 | [`packages/schema/LICENSE`](packages/schema/LICENSE) |

Copyright 2026 Nick Jordi Nouschirvan.

## What that means in practice

**The connector and the schema are ordinary open source.** Apache-2.0: use them,
fork them, ship them inside a commercial product, no obligation beyond keeping
the notices. They are deliberately frictionless, because the connector runs
inside other people's repositories and inside their build pipelines — a
non-standard license there is a question for someone's legal team, and the
answer takes weeks.

**The hub is source-available, not open source.** FSL-1.1-ALv2 permits every use
except a Competing Use: making crosscheck available to others in a commercial
product or service that substitutes for it, substitutes for something we offer
using it, or offers substantially the same functionality. Explicitly permitted
by the license text itself:

- internal use and access — running your own hub for your own team
- non-commercial education and research
- professional services you provide to someone who is themselves a licensee

So for the overwhelming majority of users the hub behaves exactly like open
source: read it, run it, modify it, contribute to it, self-host it for your
team, free of charge. What it forbids is one thing — becoming a competing
commercial offering of crosscheck itself.

**The restriction expires on its own.** Each version of the hub becomes Apache
License 2.0 on the second anniversary of the date it was made available. That
date is fixed by the license text, not by us, and it moves forward with each
release: the protection is a rolling two-year head start, not a permanent
enclosure.

## Why it is split this way

A single license cannot serve both halves. The connector needs maximum adoption
and zero legal friction; the hub is the only piece a competitor could host and
charge for, and it is the piece that runs on our own infrastructure rather than
in a customer's build. Splitting at that boundary buys the protection where it
matters and pays for it nowhere else.

This is the same shape used by Sentry, Grafana and GitLab, and for the same
reason.

## Contributing

Contributions require a Contributor License Agreement — see
[`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md) and [`CLA.md`](CLA.md). It is a one-click
step on your first pull request. It exists so the copyright stays in one place,
which is what makes the two-year conversion above, and any future relicensing,
possible at all.

## Not legal advice

This file is a plain-language summary written for readers, not a legal
instrument. Where it and a `LICENSE` file disagree, the `LICENSE` file governs.
